Task 1: Install Apache Camel
Apache Camel is the core integration framework that provides routing and mediation rules in a variety of domain-specific languages.

Subtask 1.1: Prepare the System Environment
First, let's ensure our system is updated and has the necessary prerequisites.

# Update the system packages
sudo dnf update -y

# Install Java Development Kit if not already present
sudo dnf install java-11-openjdk java-11-openjdk-devel -y

# Verify Java installation
java -version
javac -version
Subtask 1.2: Set Java Environment Variables
Configure the Java environment variables for proper operation.

# Find Java installation path
sudo find /usr/lib/jvm -name "java-11-openjdk*" -type d

# Set JAVA_HOME environment variable
echo 'export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export PATH=$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc

# Reload the environment
source ~/.bashrc

# Verify environment variables
echo $JAVA_HOME
Subtask 1.3: Download and Install Apache Camel
Download the latest stable version of Apache Camel.

# Create installation directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/apache-camel
cd /opt/apache-camel

# Download Apache Camel (version 3.21.0 - current stable)
sudo wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/camel/apache-camel/3.21.0/apache-camel-3.21.0.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
sudo tar -xzf apache-camel-3.21.0.tar.gz

# Create symbolic link for easier access
sudo ln -s apache-camel-3.21.0 current

# Set ownership and permissions
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /opt/apache-camel
Subtask 1.4: Configure Camel Environment
Set up environment variables for Apache Camel.

# Add Camel to environment variables
echo 'export CAMEL_HOME=/opt/apache-camel/current' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export PATH=$CAMEL_HOME/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc

# Reload environment
source ~/.bashrc

# Verify Camel installation
ls -la $CAMEL_HOME/lib/
Task 2: Install Apache Karaf
Apache Karaf is a lightweight OSGi container that provides a runtime environment for deploying and managing OSGi applications.

Subtask 2.1: Download Apache Karaf
# Create Karaf installation directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/apache-karaf
cd /opt/apache-karaf

# Download Apache Karaf (version 4.4.3 - current stable)
sudo wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/karaf/4.4.3/apache-karaf-4.4.3.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
sudo tar -xzf apache-karaf-4.4.3.tar.gz

# Create symbolic link
sudo ln -s apache-karaf-4.4.3 current

# Set ownership
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /opt/apache-karaf
Subtask 2.2: Configure Karaf Environment
# Set Karaf environment variables
echo 'export KARAF_HOME=/opt/apache-karaf/current' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export PATH=$KARAF_HOME/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc

# Reload environment
source ~/.bashrc

# Verify Karaf installation
ls -la $KARAF_HOME/bin/
Subtask 2.3: Configure Karaf for Fuse Integration
Edit the Karaf configuration to optimize for Fuse operations.

# Navigate to Karaf configuration directory
cd $KARAF_HOME/etc

# Backup original configuration
cp org.apache.karaf.shell.cfg org.apache.karaf.shell.cfg.backup

# Configure shell timeout (optional)
echo 'sshIdleTimeout=0' >> org.apache.karaf.shell.cfg
Task 3: Install Apache ActiveMQ
Apache ActiveMQ is a message broker that provides reliable messaging between distributed applications.

Subtask 3.1: Download and Install ActiveMQ
# Create ActiveMQ installation directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/apache-activemq
cd /opt/apache-activemq

# Download Apache ActiveMQ (version 5.18.2 - current stable)
sudo wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/activemq/5.18.2/apache-activemq-5.18.2-bin.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
sudo tar -xzf apache-activemq-5.18.2-bin.tar.gz

# Create symbolic link
sudo ln -s apache-activemq-5.18.2 current

# Set ownership
sudo chown -R $USER:$USER /opt/apache-activemq
Subtask 3.2: Configure ActiveMQ Environment
# Set ActiveMQ environment variables
echo 'export ACTIVEMQ_HOME=/opt/apache-activemq/current' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export PATH=$ACTIVEMQ_HOME/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc

# Reload environment
source ~/.bashrc

# Verify ActiveMQ installation
ls -la $ACTIVEMQ_HOME/bin/
Subtask 3.3: Configure ActiveMQ Settings
Configure ActiveMQ for optimal performance and security.

# Navigate to ActiveMQ configuration
cd $ACTIVEMQ_HOME/conf

# Backup original configuration
cp activemq.xml activemq.xml.backup

# Create a basic configuration file
cat > activemq.xml << 'EOF'
<beans
  xmlns="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans/spring-beans.xsd
  http://activemq.apache.org/schema/core http://activemq.apache.org/schema/core/activemq-core.xsd">

    <bean class="org.springframework.beans.factory.config.PropertyPlaceholderConfigurer">
        <property name="locations">
            <value>file:${activemq.conf}/credentials.properties</value>
        </property>
    </bean>

    <broker xmlns="http://activemq.apache.org/schema/core" brokerName="localhost" dataDirectory="${activemq.data}">
        <destinationPolicy>
            <policyMap>
              <policyEntries>
                <policyEntry topic=">" >
                  <pendingMessageLimitStrategy>
                    <constantPendingMessageLimitStrategy limit="1000"/>
                  </pendingMessageLimitStrategy>
                </policyEntry>
              </policyEntries>
            </policyMap>
        </destinationPolicy>

        <managementContext>
            <managementContext createConnector="false"/>
        </managementContext>

        <persistenceAdapter>
            <kahaDB directory="${activemq.data}/kahadb"/>
        </persistenceAdapter>

        <transportConnectors>
            <transportConnector name="openwire" uri="tcp://0.0.0.0:61616?maximumConnections=1000&amp;wireFormat.maxFrameSize=104857600"/>
            <transportConnector name="amqp" uri="amqp://0.0.0.0:5672?maximumConnections=1000&amp;wireFormat.maxFrameSize=104857600"/>
        </transportConnectors>

        <shutdownHooks>
            <bean xmlns="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans" class="org.apache.activemq.hooks.SpringContextHook" />
        </shutdownHooks>
    </broker>

    <import resource="jetty.xml"/>
</beans>
EOF
Task 4: Verify Installation by Checking Service Status
Now let's verify that all components are properly installed and can be started.

Subtask 4.1: Test Apache Camel Installation
# Check if Camel libraries are accessible
ls -la $CAMEL_HOME/lib/ | grep camel-core

# Verify Camel version
find $CAMEL_HOME/lib -name "camel-core-*.jar" -exec basename {} \;
Subtask 4.2: Test Apache Karaf Installation
# Start Karaf in background mode
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/start

# Wait a few seconds for startup
sleep 10

# Check if Karaf is running
./bin/status

# Connect to Karaf console to verify
./bin/client "version"

# Stop Karaf for now
./bin/stop
Subtask 4.3: Test Apache ActiveMQ Installation
# Start ActiveMQ in background
cd $ACTIVEMQ_HOME
./bin/activemq start

# Wait for startup
sleep 15

# Check ActiveMQ status
./bin/activemq status

# Verify ActiveMQ is listening on correct ports
netstat -tlnp | grep :61616
netstat -tlnp | grep :8161

# Stop ActiveMQ for now
./bin/activemq stop
Task 5: Start the Fuse Environment and Access the Console
Now let's start all components together to create a complete Fuse environment.

Subtask 5.1: Create Startup Scripts
Create convenient scripts to start all services together.

# Create a startup script directory
mkdir -p ~/fuse-scripts

# Create the startup script
cat > ~/fuse-scripts/start-fuse.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Starting Red Hat Fuse Environment..."

# Start ActiveMQ
echo "Starting ActiveMQ..."
cd $ACTIVEMQ_HOME
./bin/activemq start

# Wait for ActiveMQ to start
sleep 10

# Start Karaf
echo "Starting Karaf..."
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/start

# Wait for Karaf to start
sleep 15

echo "Fuse environment started successfully!"
echo "ActiveMQ Web Console: http://localhost:8161/admin (admin/admin)"
echo "Karaf Console: $KARAF_HOME/bin/client"
EOF

# Create the shutdown script
cat > ~/fuse-scripts/stop-fuse.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Stopping Red Hat Fuse Environment..."

# Stop Karaf
echo "Stopping Karaf..."
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/stop

# Stop ActiveMQ
echo "Stopping ActiveMQ..."
cd $ACTIVEMQ_HOME
./bin/activemq stop

echo "Fuse environment stopped successfully!"
EOF

# Make scripts executable
chmod +x ~/fuse-scripts/*.sh
Subtask 5.2: Start the Complete Fuse Environment
# Run the startup script
~/fuse-scripts/start-fuse.sh

# Verify all services are running
echo "Checking service status..."

# Check ActiveMQ
cd $ACTIVEMQ_HOME
./bin/activemq status

# Check Karaf
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/status
Subtask 5.3: Access Management Consoles
ActiveMQ Web Console Access:

# Check if ActiveMQ web console is accessible
curl -I http://localhost:8161/admin/

# If successful, you can access it via browser at:
# http://localhost:8161/admin/
# Default credentials: admin/admin
Karaf Console Access:

# Connect to Karaf console
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/client

# Once connected, try these commands:
# version
# list
# feature:list | grep camel
# exit
Subtask 5.4: Install Camel Features in Karaf
Connect to Karaf and install Camel features for complete integration.

# Connect to Karaf console
cd $KARAF_HOME
./bin/client

# In the Karaf console, run these commands:
# feature:repo-add camel 3.21.0
